# JavaScript / component style

Write ES6+ TypeScript. Never `var`. Prefer `const`; use `let` only when the binding is reassigned.

Use arrow functions, destructuring, object/array spread, optional chaining, nullish coalescing, and `async`/`await` instead of `function` callbacks, `Object.assign`, or `.then()` chains.

Files: **kebab-case** (`patient-form.tsx`, `use-create-patient.ts`).

## Components (`.tsx`)

One component per file. Typed props, arrow function, default export:

```tsx
type Props = {
  label: string;
  onPress: () => void;
};

const PrimaryButton = ({ label, onPress }: Props) => {
  return null;
};

export default PrimaryButton;
```

If the file exports more than one component, name the type `{ComponentName}Props` instead of `Props`.

Next.js `page.tsx` / `layout.tsx` stay default exports (required). Keep them thin — import the feature screen as default:

```tsx
import TodayBoard from "@/features/today-board/today-board";

const TodayPage = () => <TodayBoard />;

export default TodayPage;
```

`route.ts` uses named HTTP handlers (`GET`, `POST`).

## Not components

Named exports for domain, Dexie helpers, hooks, and Zod (`export const createPatient = …`, `export const useCreatePatient = () => { … }`).
